---
const repo = 'https://github.com/0xHelico/helico'
// Reading about Helico goes to the docs site; looking at the code goes to GitHub.
const docs = 'https://docs.helico.site/docs'
const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'What is Helico, in plain words?',
		a: 'Money sitting in a wallet earns nothing. Money you have lent out cannot be spent. Helico keeps the balance between the two: an agent moves idle funds into a lending market you allow-listed, and pulls them back when you need cash. You set the terms once. How much stays spendable, which markets it may use, and which agent may act. The account holding your money cannot send it anywhere else, because the code has no way to.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What do I need to start?',
		a: 'A wallet and some stablecoins on Arbitrum. You get an account of your own at an address that is known before it is built, so you can fund it before it exists, and whatever arrives there was always yours. Five contracts are deployed on Arbitrum One with their source published. Nobody has opened an account yet, so what we can show you today is the code, those deployments, and a rehearsal on a copy of the market.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Who holds my money?',
		a: 'An account that is yours and nobody else\'s. One contract per owner, not a pool everyone shares. It has a way out that no upgrade can remove: a function on the account that sends everything back to you and answers to nobody else. When it trades, the tokens go from your account to the buyer directly. The exchange keeps a record of what may be spent and never holds anything itself.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What can the automation do to it?',
		a: 'Two things. Put idle money into a market you allow-listed, and take it back out. Neither call has anywhere to put an address, so there is no way to write an instruction that sends your money somewhere else. An agent that was completely taken over could shuffle your money between the markets you chose, paying its own gas to do it, and nothing more.',
	},
	{
		q: 'When does it do nothing?',
		a: 'Most of the time. Interest builds every block, so the balance drifts constantly, and chasing it would mean a transaction every few minutes to move a rounding error. A move has to be worth making twice over: big enough that gas is not most of it, and big enough to matter against the size of your account. Small accounts and large ones are stopped by different halves of that.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What stays private?',
		a: 'How the decision is made. The program that decides runs inside a sealed environment. Whose funds it watches, when it looks, and how it sizes a move never leave that environment. The rules themselves are public on the chain, so anyone can check that a move followed them.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Is it live? Is it audited?',
		a: 'Live, but not audited, and nobody is using it yet. Five contracts are deployed on Arbitrum One with their source published, and the program that decides runs on Chainlink\'s network every five minutes. Nobody has opened an account, so it has nothing to manage and holds on every run. The whole flow has been run end to end on a copy of the real market: an account opened at an address predicted before it existed, real stablecoins moved into the real lending market, and the decision made inside the sealed environment. That copy was not the live network, and that sealed environment was simulated. Twelve AI reviewers went over the contracts, then six more. That is not a professional audit, and this page does not call it one.',
	},
]
---

<section class="wrap faq" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title" data-reveal>
	<h2 id="faq-title">FAQs</h2>
	<div class="faq__list">
		{faqs.map((f) => (
			<details class="faq__item">
				<summary>
					<span>{f.q}</span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14" /><path d="M5 12h14" /></svg>
				</summary>
				<p>{f.a}</p>
			</details>
		))}
	</div>
	<p class="faq__more"><a href={`${docs}/introduction`}>Read the Helico docs</a></p>
</section>

<style>
	.faq {
		max-width: 662px;
		margin-top: clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem);
		margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
	}
	.faq h2 {
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
		font-weight: 500;
		line-height: 1.35;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
		color: var(--ink-90);
	}
	.faq__item {
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(37, 34, 40, 0.08);
	}
	.faq__item summary {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 1rem;
		padding: 1.4rem 0;
		cursor: pointer;
		list-style: none;
		font-size: 1rem;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		color: var(--ink-90);
	}
	.faq__item summary::-webkit-details-marker {
		display: none;
	}
	.faq__item summary svg {
		flex: none;
		width: 1rem;
		height: 1rem;
		color: var(--ink-65);
		transition: transform 0.2s;
	}
	.faq__item[open] summary svg {
		transform: rotate(45deg);
	}
	/* The answer unfolds downward. Browsers without ::details-content open it instantly. */
	.faq {
		interpolate-size: allow-keywords;
	}
	.faq__item::details-content {
		height: 0;
		overflow: clip;
		transition:
			height 0.3s ease,
			content-visibility 0.3s allow-discrete;
	}
	.faq__item[open]::details-content {
		height: auto;
	}
	.faq__item[open] p {
		animation: faq-in 0.3s ease;
	}
	@keyframes faq-in {
		from {
			opacity: 0;
			transform: translateY(-0.35rem);
		}
	}
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.faq__item::details-content {
			transition: none;
		}
		.faq__item[open] p {
			animation: none;
		}
	}
	.faq__item p {
		margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.5;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		color: var(--ink-65);
	}
	.faq__more {
		margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		font-weight: 450;
	}
	.faq__more a {
		color: var(--lav-text);
	}
</style>
